WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.169
Hello everybody.

00:00:04.169 --> 00:00:05.220
Welcome to the webinar.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.010
This is Peter Cooper.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:12.050
This is the insider's guide to the new My
Bibliography.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:17.190
With me today are Sherry Bailey and Rana Morris,
and Sherry is going to be our presenter today.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:23.180
Today you will receive a lot of information
on how to navigate this new system.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:26.180
I want to thank each of you for joining us.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:33.059
Thank you for your patience as we go through
these growing pains together.

00:00:33.059 --> 00:00:40.929
I am from NCBI user services and I am your
advocate in providing your feedback, suggestions,

00:00:40.929 --> 00:00:43.420
and reporting any issues.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:49.289
With that said take a moment to write down
the email address to provide your suggestions

00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:54.600
and feedback on what we are doing right and
how we can improve.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.329
info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

00:00:56.329 --> 00:00:57.519
Let's get started.

00:00:57.519 --> 00:01:03.420
For those of you who are new to My NCBI, I
want to quickly go over reasons why you should

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.790
get an account.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:12.549
We have videos on our YouTube channel and
on-line manuals which provide step-by-step

00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:16.289
directions for each of these tools.

00:01:16.289 --> 00:01:22.570
The main reason why we are here today is to
discuss creating and managing citations within

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:27.359
My Bibliography.

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:35.369
One of the easiest ways to log into your account
is to directly go to PubMed.gov.

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:44.079
If you don't already have an account Go to
PubMed.gov click register for an account,

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:51.830
follow the prompts, click the activation link
which is sent to the email address, now you're

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:56.060
ready to log in.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.609
We have officially logged into my NCBI.

00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:05.570
On the dashboard there are many different
portlets such as the ability to have your

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:15.599
saved searches run automatically, create collections
that you can share, create custom filters,

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:23.430
create bio sketches, and see your recent activity
as long as you are loged into your account

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.950
at the time of your searches.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:31.540
Let's get back to My Bibliography which is
accessed by clicking the manage My Bibliography

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.290
link.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:38.080
Once you have citations, you may see a spinning
icon.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.599
Do not wait for it to finish.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:47.590
Go ahead click manage My Bibliography.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.800
Now we are at the My Bibliography page.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:58.610
On the upper right hand side, if you click
the username, you will find the login information,

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:06.430
the dashboard which we just came from, publications
in My Bibliography which is the screen we

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:14.941
are on, account settings, here's where you
can update your password, email address, link

00:03:14.941 --> 00:03:22.620
accounts like the institution's credentials,
ERA commons HRA, and Google to name a few.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.049
You have the ability to invite others to manage
your account.

00:03:27.049 --> 00:03:30.239
Of course, you can log out.

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:36.909
Next in the gray [blue] box, you can see the
name listed on your account.

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:42.930
In this case the first name is ncbi and the
last name is user.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:52.610
Beneath the gray box if you have an award
such as eRA commons for grants, or HRA grants,

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:54.910
icons will appear here.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:04:01.459
This is another way to verify that your award
information is correctly linked to your account.

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:06.939
All new accounts are automatically set to
private.

00:04:06.939 --> 00:04:13.420
If you wish to share your bibliography with
others, click on the make your bibliography

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:19.150
public link, to reveal the public URL.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:25.660
You have the option to toggle the link on
and off if you wish you restrict viewing.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:31.300
To share or not to share, is completely under
your control.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:38.090
Before we go any further, if you run into
any issues the support center is a click away.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:45.260
You will find self-help articles and the ability
to contact the helpdesk.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.630
Let's add a few citations.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:53.360
The first option is from PubMed.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.880
There are numerous ways to search PubMed.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:03.030
For example, you can run and author search
by using the full name or last name and initials,

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:07.860
or even a partial title as I entered here.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:14.510
Click Search PubMed, you have the results
and in this case it is one result.

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:17.290
This is the exact title I was looking for.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:21.950
I will check the box and add that to the bibliography.

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:28.320
I will then have a confirmation saying it
was successfully added as well as my checkbox

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.310
is grayed out.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:38.660
From here I can repeat the process as many
times as I want, then exit out.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.910
You may also add citations from a file.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:52.020
The file must be in the RIS format most reference
managers can convert the citations to RIS.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:56.900
As you can see you're given a confirmation
box on how many records have been uploaded

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.440
and updated.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.770
Last but not least, is adding citations manually.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:11.530
You have the ability to add citations of articles
not appearing in PubMed, books,patents, software

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:17.780
and other citations.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:23.030
We have a few citations let's manage them.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:28.620
First we will go over the features of both
types of accounts that they have in common,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:34.840
meaning, those accounts that are linked to
granting agencies like eRA Commons and HRA

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:42.080
and those that are not .In Manage citations,
there are two different types of drop downs,

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:48.020
the account that is private, meaning I have
not activated my public URL, which is seen

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:54.520
here, and accounts that show the public URL.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:07:01.210
The difference is set to public and set to
private.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:07.300
Public citations which the title implies can
be seen in the public URL.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:15.550
The private label is for those that you do
not want to be seen in the public URL.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:17.610
Let me give you an example.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:23.800
A PI who is not the author on a manuscript
but the award funded the manuscript will use

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:30.920
the private label which is seen on the first
citation, for the citation.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:39.580
Yes, those of you who had an account previously,
the private label has taken the place of other

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:41.570
citations.

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:47.910
The PI is responsible for reporting the grant
but only wants to see items they authored

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:49.510
in the public URL.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:53.540
Otherwise, you will use the private label.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:08:01.090
Back to manage citations, we have copied to
a collection This is a great option if you

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:06.970
wish to have separate collections to share
without sharing your entire bibliography.

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:11.890
You can add citations to this collection as
seen here.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:19.860
Only PubMed citations can be copied into this
collection.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:28.730
Finally you can delete citations, you can
export some or all of the citations to a RIS

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:29.770
file.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:32.490
Back to the homepage.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:35.879
You may also at times encounter a warning
message.

00:08:35.879 --> 00:08:42.269
This occurs when there is either duplicate
citations or you have a citation that may

00:08:42.269 --> 00:08:45.480
match a citation appearing in PubMed.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:51.660
You have two choices, you can hide the warning
or you can resolve it.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:57.589
To resolve the the conflict, click the conflict
link determine if the citations are the same,

00:08:57.589 --> 00:09:02.170
not on the list or save the decision for another
day.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:06.149
In this particular case, the citations are
the same.

00:09:06.149 --> 00:09:10.579
Just the author names are out of order.

00:09:10.579 --> 00:09:15.180
What happens if you accidentally dismissed
the warning box or you discover duplicate

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:16.180
citations?

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:19.070
It's an easy fix.

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:22.000
Step number one, locate duplicate citation.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.330
Write down the PMID number.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:27.750
You need this later.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:34.610
Step number two, click edit citation of the
manual citation.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:42.230
A pop up box will appear, locate the type
field and use the drop-down box and select

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:44.139
PMID.

00:09:44.139 --> 00:09:52.779
Next in unique ID, you will write down or
type in excuse me, the PMID number.

00:09:52.779 --> 00:09:58.160
You will receive confirmation box verifying
that you have the right citation, then you

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.070
simply click, yes.

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:03.279
We have one problem solved.

00:10:03.279 --> 00:10:08.850
Let's move on to the right side of the screen
for filtering and sorting citations.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:17.620
The search box located on the upper right
with this you can search by PMCID, PMID, article

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:24.209
title, whole or partial, author name, DOI
or journal title.

00:10:24.209 --> 00:10:32.790
Let me repeat that again, you can search by
PubMed ID, PubMed Central ID, article titles,

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:38.920
whole or partial, author name, DOI and journal
title.

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:46.370
May also sort the bibliography by recently
updated citations meaning, recently added

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:48.350
to your account.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:55.190
By publication date, or by author name in
descending or ascending order.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:59.720
So far we have reviewed what the accounts
have in common.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:04.870
Let's take a quick look at accounts that have
linked awards.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:11.749
Under manage citations, there is manage awards,
and the ability to create a compliance report.

00:11:11.749 --> 00:11:18.850
By using the manage awards link you can quickly
add awards to multiple citations and/or multiple

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:21.220
awards to citations.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.180
Let's see this in action.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:31.240
First select the citations here, and then
you will go to manage citation assign awards,

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:36.319
you will assign the awards and you have the
ability to search for other words if the awards

00:11:36.319 --> 00:11:39.779
are not your own.

00:11:39.779 --> 00:11:47.889
In this particular one we selected two awards,
and the information has been saved, verified

00:11:47.889 --> 00:11:48.889
here.

00:11:48.889 --> 00:11:52.120
We will close the citation window.

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:56.769
Let's take a look at the first citation that
we selected.

00:11:56.769 --> 00:12:03.500
The count did not change, because one of the
awards was the same so instead of three, I

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:05.120
have two.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:09.050
Meaning that I selected the same award twice.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:12.569
It will automatically collapse.

00:12:12.569 --> 00:12:17.100
Before we create a compliance report, let's
talk about compliance.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:24.709
I'm sure you noticed the boxes on the side,
and they also correspond to the icons in the

00:12:24.709 --> 00:12:27.630
gray box above.

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:32.079
The question Mark means that public access
is not defined.

00:12:32.079 --> 00:12:36.899
The exclamation point lets you know the citation
is noncompliant.

00:12:36.899 --> 00:12:43.999
The arrow indicates the citation is in process
and receiving a PMC ID.

00:12:43.999 --> 00:12:51.220
The green checkmark indicates it has received
the PubMed central ID and is in compliance.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:56.199
Let's say you wish to see citations that are
in process.

00:12:56.199 --> 00:13:01.580
Simply click on the blue arrow located in
the gray box above, to display only those

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:05.100
citations.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:09.220
Our grants are attached and we are ready to
create a compliance report.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:13.620
To start, we will select filter citations.

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:24.379
Next we will select the grant, select the
citations, go to manage citations, click compliance

00:13:24.379 --> 00:13:33.459
report, fill out the form which includes first
name, middle name, last name, starting page

00:13:33.459 --> 00:13:42.209
number, if applicable, and you click download
the PDF, which appears here.

00:13:42.209 --> 00:13:47.319
One of the last features I want to show you
today is the ability to sort all of your records

00:13:47.319 --> 00:13:49.079
by public access.

00:13:49.079 --> 00:13:52.759
Which is also found in the sorting field.

00:13:52.759 --> 00:13:57.709
If you have any remaining questions take a
moment to type them out into the chat box

00:13:57.709 --> 00:14:00.949
because this concludes our tour of My Bibliography.

00:14:00.949 --> 00:14:04.499
I have one last thing to show you.

00:14:04.499 --> 00:14:11.350
Coming soon is a more advanced filtering,
which will be available in about two weeks

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:12.769
or so.

00:14:12.769 --> 00:14:19.160
Grant holders can filter by multiple grants
and everyone can filter citations by citation

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:23.379
type, public and private.

00:14:23.379 --> 00:14:27.070
This is my final request before we take questions.

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:30.189
We need your help to make our product better.

00:14:30.189 --> 00:14:34.740
As you think of features you and others can
benefit from please write us.

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:35.740
info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:40.060
I want to thank you again for joining me,
and on behalf of the NCBI User Services we

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:41.579
look forward to hearing from you.

00:14:41.579 --> 00:14:48.649
[Rana Morris] Hi Sherry, this is a pretty
common question that came in fairly early.

00:14:48.649 --> 00:14:57.060
As you make updates to the website to My Bibliography
and other accounts, are their accounts and

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.920
account information automatically transferred?

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:04.560
Do we need to worry about updating the account
every time the user interfaces change?

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:06.170
[Sherri Bailey] They do not.

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:10.990
Everything should be in its proper place even
though the interface may change.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:17.110
[Rana Morris] Another question, Is there a
plan to make a change in the number of items

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:21.329
displayed per page or it will always be listed
as is.

00:15:21.329 --> 00:15:25.250
[Sherri Bailey] That's a perfet suggestion.

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:29.200
Use the email on the screen now.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:30.790
info@ncbi.nlmnih.gov
Please suggest it.

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:33.690
We make changes based on user needs.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:40.679
[Rana Morris] There are a number of librarians
online who do training for My bib, SciEnCV,

00:15:40.679 --> 00:15:44.050
and public access compliance.

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:48.079
They want to create slide sets for the presentations.

00:15:48.079 --> 00:15:55.889
They want to know if it is possible to get
a fake account so they can create those slides?

00:15:55.889 --> 00:15:58.610
What is a reasonable way to do that?

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:03.950
[Sherri Bailey] write into the email address
you see and we can check with the development

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:05.980
team to see what we can do.

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:13.330
[Rana Morris] They are interested and excited
about the changes, and want to know if there

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:17.379
are plans to improve SciEnCV since they are
connected?

00:16:17.379 --> 00:16:19.379
[Sherri Bailey] Stay tuned.

00:16:19.379 --> 00:16:22.679
It is happening and will be coming in the
near future.

00:16:22.679 --> 00:16:30.720
[Rana Morris] There are a number of suggestions
that have come in.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:38.029
Other people have mentioned that they do training
and have validated some of the other features

00:16:38.029 --> 00:16:44.529
and we will take all of those and bring them
to the team to see what they can do.

00:16:44.529 --> 00:16:50.820
Are you updating the my NCBI Help with information
about making the citations public and private?

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:51.820
[Sherri Bailey] Yes.

00:16:51.820 --> 00:17:03.139
It should be available now if you go to the
My Bibliography documentation, it should show

00:17:03.139 --> 00:17:06.420
all the current screenshots that I showed
you today.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:07.420
www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK53595

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:13.010
[Rana Morris] If the citations are appearing
where the user is a PI but not an author,

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:18.240
they are on a grant but not an author, can
you go over that one more time?

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:21.130
What do you set private?

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:29.050
[Sherri Bailey] If the URL is already public,
it will show everything there.

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:36.620
A PI who is not the author on the grant what
you want to do is select those citations,

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:45.240
go to manage citations, you want to set those
citations to private so it will not show in

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:46.630
the public URL.

00:17:46.630 --> 00:17:52.310
[Rana Morris] Is there currently a limit that
we know about for the number of citations

00:17:52.310 --> 00:17:54.540
that can be held in My Bib?

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:57.630
[Sherri Bailey] Great question.

00:17:57.630 --> 00:18:02.970
It is part of the reason why we updated to
My Bibliography 3.0.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:06.780
There is not a limit currently, you will have
multiple pages.

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:12.700
[Rana Morris] That will help the researchers
who have hundreds and hundreds of citations.

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:19.650
Is there any intention to add additional features
in addition to the filter feature you showed

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.080
for specific grants?

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:28.680
Are there other sort options or features that
you are thinking about or would you consider

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:32.370
taking in additional sort or feature references?

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:36.030
For sort, maybe things like most relevant
to grants, etc.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:39.310
[Sherri Bailey] This is why we ask for your
feedback.

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:47.010
We want to make this product as usable to
you not just what we think you need.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:52.640
This is why we ask for feedback.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:56.940
We want to make this product the best it can
be to fit your needs.

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:00.180
[Rana Morris] Here is another question about
public and private.

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:05.790
It sounds like something that would be good
to address, not only in FAQs but in documentation

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:07.010
on the system.

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:11.740
One of the librarians says this is happening
to a client of mine.

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:17.670
NIH told him he needs to remove citations
appearing in My Bibliography for those which

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:19.270
he is not an author.

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:22.360
Is that what the private thing is supposed
to do?

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:27.430
By identifying them as private, remove them
from the citation.

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:32.190
[Sherri Bailey] It removes it not from the
citation list.

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:33.630
Everything will be there.

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:36.930
Underneath the citation itself it will say
private.

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:44.730
If the PI or the person uses the actual link,
the public URL, the only thing they will see

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:48.130
are those things that are considered public.

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:50.450
Anything with a private label will be hidden.

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:55.810
[Rana Morris] We used to be able to select
the view citations in PubMed.

00:19:55.810 --> 00:19:57.350
This is a useful feature.

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:05.400
The citations could be sent to EndNote, will
you add this feature back to My Bibliography?

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:07.000
Or is there another way to do it?

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:13.320
[Sherri Bailey] There's not another way to
do that, if you can please write that down

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:17.550
and stick that in our suggestions, we would
appreciate it.

00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:21.790
[Rana Morris] There are a lot of suggestions.

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:27.450
We will compile all of these and send them
in to the team.

